# TCP/IP Model

The Internet primarily uses the TCP/IP model.

| TCP/IP Layer   | OSI Equivalent                       |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Application    | Application + Presentation + Session |
| Transport      | Transport                            |
| Internet       | Network                              |
| Network Access | Data Link + Physical                 |

This simplified model is what you'll encounter most often in real-world networking.

---

# 🌍 Real-World Example

When you visit your bank's website:

1. Browser starts the request (Application Layer).
2. Data is encrypted (Presentation Layer).
3. A session is established.
4. TCP ensures reliable delivery.
5. IP routes packets to the bank's server.
6. Ethernet or Wi-Fi carries the frames.
7. Electrical/radio signals transmit the data.

---

# 🔹 Level 3 – Advanced Concepts

### Why Attackers Target Different Layers

* **Layer 7:** SQL Injection, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
* **Layer 4:** TCP SYN Flood (DoS attack)
* **Layer 3:** IP Spoofing
* **Layer 2:** ARP Spoofing
* **Layer 1:** Physical cable tampering

Defenders must secure every layer, not just one.

---

# Common Beginner Mistakes

❌ Memorizing layer names without understanding their roles.

❌ Confusing MAC addresses with IP addresses.

❌ Thinking routers and switches perform the same function.

❌ Ignoring TCP vs. UDP differences.

